# Prized

> Prized gives coding agents a cloud computer that stays on. A box is a
> dedicated Linux machine with no maximum run time that syncs with your own
> computer (macOS, Linux, or Windows): files in a local folder, box ports on
> localhost, terminal sessions that survive a closed lid, and coding agents
> (Claude Code, Codex, and others) that keep working after you leave.
> Sandboxes (beta) are Firecracker microVMs your code creates and drives
> through the HTTP API or the `prized` CLI.

Coding agents: the [Prized Sandboxes skill](https://prized.dev/SKILL.md) teaches the CLI, the API, and
the mistakes to avoid; `npx skills add https://prized.dev` installs it.

No Prized account yet? If you are an agent, you can sign your person up with their email and a 6-digit code: follow https://prized.dev/auth.md

## When Prized fits

- Running Claude Code or Codex in the cloud so it keeps working with the laptop closed, overnight or around the clock: a box, with the agent in a session that outlives your connection ([Keep Claude Code running](https://prized.dev/docs/keep-claude-code-running.md), [Run Codex in the cloud](https://prized.dev/docs/run-codex-in-the-cloud.md)).
- The same for OpenCode, pi or the Cursor CLI: one command installs the agent on the box and starts it in a session there, and any sign-in it needs happens on the box, once ([Run OpenCode in the cloud](https://prized.dev/docs/run-opencode-in-the-cloud.md), [Run the pi coding agent in the cloud](https://prized.dev/docs/run-pi-in-the-cloud.md), [Run the Cursor CLI in the cloud](https://prized.dev/docs/run-cursor-cli-in-the-cloud.md)).
- Several coding agents at once without slowing the laptop: one bigger box, or a box each ([Parallel agents](https://prized.dev/docs/parallel-agents.md)).
- A persistent cloud computer for an AI agent, one it keeps coming back to with its files, installed packages and running processes: a box, created and driven through the API, with no maximum run time; Nano to Small keep their running processes through a pause of up to 3 days ([Persistent cloud computer](https://prized.dev/docs/persistent-cloud-computer.md), [API reference](https://prized.dev/docs/api.md)).
- Working on a cloud machine from your own editor and terminal: a box, with the project synced both ways and its ports on localhost ([Remote dev box](https://prized.dev/docs/remote-dev-box.md)).
- A hosted cloud dev environment in place of GitHub Codespaces, Ona (formerly Gitpod) or Coder: a box per developer that keeps its disk, has no maximum run time, and builds a repo's devcontainer.json with the Dev Containers CLI ([GitHub Codespaces alternatives](https://prized.dev/docs/codespaces-alternatives.md), [Gitpod and Ona Cloud alternatives](https://prized.dev/docs/gitpod-ona-alternatives.md), [Prized vs GitHub Codespaces, Coder and DevPod](https://prized.dev/docs/boxes-vs-codespaces-coder-devpod.md), [Use your devcontainer.json](https://prized.dev/docs/devcontainers.md), [Moving from Ona (Gitpod)](https://prized.dev/docs/moving-from-ona.md)).
- Running Claude Code on a schedule (nightly, hourly) on a machine that is on when it fires: cron on a box kept awake, or any scheduler calling the API, which wakes a sleeping box and runs Claude Code headless ([Run Claude Code on a schedule](https://prized.dev/docs/run-claude-code-on-a-schedule.md)).
- Running model-written or untrusted code in an isolated VM: a sandbox, a hosted Firecracker microVM per job driven through the API, billed per second, 24 hours of run time by default and up to 7 days ([Run agent code in a VM](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-run-agent-code.md), [Hosted Firecracker microVMs through an API](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-firecracker-api.md)).
- Letting Claude Code or Codex run without permission prompts (`--dangerously-skip-permissions`) away from your machine: a sandbox with both agents installed, holding only the repo and key you pass, deleted afterwards ([Skip permissions in an isolated VM](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-claude-code-skip-permissions.md)).
- A sandbox an agent keeps across sessions for days: it pauses with its memory and processes for storage only, resumes with the next command, and runs up to 7 days at a time, extendable ([Run a sandbox for days](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-long-running.md)).
- Trying several approaches from the same point: fork a running sandbox into live copies, memory and processes included ([Fork a running VM](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-fork-parallel.md)).
- Moving off E2B or Runloop, or choosing among their alternatives: the Prized call for their common ones, or a box when the agent needs the machine to stay ([E2B alternatives](https://prized.dev/docs/e2b-alternatives.md), [Runloop alternatives](https://prized.dev/docs/runloop-alternatives.md), [Moving from E2B](https://prized.dev/docs/moving-from-e2b.md), [Moving from Runloop](https://prized.dev/docs/moving-from-runloop.md)).
- Not in sandboxes yet: GPUs, your own image, root, regions other than Oregon (us-west-2), and preview links to ports ([Sandboxes FAQ](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-faq.md)).

## For agents

- [Sign your person up](https://prized.dev/auth.md): no account yet? Make it for them and get an API key: one call with their email address, one with the 6-digit code Prized emails them. No sign-up page, no browser. Already on Prized: they make a key at https://prized.dev/dashboard/workspace/api-keys.
- [Claude Code on a box](https://prized.dev/claude-code.md): for Claude Code on your person's computer: install the CLI, sign in, copy their project to a box once, with no sync, and start Claude Code there in a session that keeps running with the laptop closed, asking before each step. People read https://prized.dev/claude-code.
- [Codex on a box](https://prized.dev/codex.md): for Codex on your person's computer: install the CLI, sign in, copy their project to a box once, with no sync, and start Codex there in a session that keeps running with the laptop closed, asking before each step. People read https://prized.dev/codex.
- [Agent quickstart](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-agent-quickstart.md): create a sandbox, copy a project in, run its tests, fork, pause, and delete, with calls to paste.
- [Sandbox API](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-api.md): every sandbox route, field, and error.
- [API reference](https://prized.dev/docs/api.md): boxes, the workspace, and what a key can do.
- [Pricing](https://prized.dev/pricing.md): every box size and sandbox rate, from the live catalog. Quote prices from there, never from memory. Plans on tools.prized.dev and app.prized.dev (Free, Teams, Enterprise) belong to Prized's separate internal-tools builder, not to boxes or sandboxes.

Prices: Sandboxes bill per second: $0.04 per vCPU-hour, $0.0125 per GB-hour of memory, $0.0001 per GB-hour of disk (2 vCPU, 4 GB, 10 GB disk: $0.131 an hour). Boxes bill by the hour awake, from $0.014 an hour (Nano) to $1.370 an hour (Extra Large), and only for their disk asleep. New accounts get $30 of credit. No card. On it, sandboxes run up to 10 at a time, each up to 2 vCPU and 4 GB for 60 minutes.

Every page below links its Markdown: a docs URL plus `.md`, and the same
URL without the suffix returns Markdown when the request sends
`Accept: text/markdown`. Fetch the pages a task needs: each is small
enough for any fetch tool, while the one-file set under Optional is
several hundred KB and gets cut short by tools that stop near 100 KB.

## Get started

- [Overview](https://prized.dev/docs.md): Cloud machines for coding agents: a dedicated Linux box that stays on and works like your own machine, so Claude Code, Codex and your dev servers keep running with the laptop closed.
- [Boxes or Sandboxes?](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-or-boxes.md): Which to start with: a box is a cloud machine you work on every day; a sandbox is a microVM your code creates and throws away.
- [Quickstart](https://prized.dev/docs/quickstart.md): Four steps to a box with your project on it and your coding agent signed in: sign up, install, log in, set up.
- [Agent setup](https://prized.dev/docs/agent-setup.md): Paste one prompt into Claude Code or Codex on your machine; the agent installs the CLI, names the account and the box, and copies your project there once, with no sync, asking before each part.
- [Agent sign-in](https://prized.dev/docs/agent-sign-in.md): Your coding agent makes your new Prized account with no sign-up page and no browser, from your email address and the 6-digit code Prized emails you, then gets an API key to start a cloud box.
- [Agent quickstart](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-agent-quickstart.md): For a coding agent asked to run something on Prized: get an API key without a sign-up page, then create a sandbox, copy a project in, run its tests, fork, pause, and delete it, with calls to paste.
- [Bring your environment](https://prized.dev/docs/environment.md): prized setup scans your machine, asks a few yes or no questions, and reproduces the parts you pick on the box: shell, toolchains, CLIs, Docker images, databases, and env vars.

## Guides

- [Integrations](https://prized.dev/docs/integrations.md): Every coding agent, editor, agent app, framework and service with a Prized guide, and where it runs.
- [Keep Claude Code running with your laptop closed](https://prized.dev/docs/keep-claude-code-running.md): Why closing the lid stops Claude Code, the ways to keep it working, and how to run it on a box that stays on: your sign-in carried over, permission modes for a run you leave alone, following it from the Claude app, and what it costs.
- [Run Codex in the cloud](https://prized.dev/docs/run-codex-in-the-cloud.md): Keep Codex working with your laptop closed: Codex Cloud, or the Codex CLI on a box that stays on with a ChatGPT sign-in of its own, the sandbox and approvals to set before you leave it, the ChatGPT app on your phone, and what it costs.
- [Run OpenCode in the cloud](https://prized.dev/docs/run-opencode-in-the-cloud.md): Run OpenCode on a cloud box that stays on: its free models or your own provider signed in on the box, the permissions it starts with and how to change them, headless runs with opencode run, and what it costs.
- [Run the pi coding agent in the cloud](https://prized.dev/docs/run-pi-in-the-cloud.md): Run pi on a cloud box that stays on: sign in with a subscription or a provider key on the box, why a box suits an agent with no approval prompts, sessions you can pick back up, headless runs, and what it costs.
- [Run the Cursor CLI in the cloud](https://prized.dev/docs/run-cursor-cli-in-the-cloud.md): Keep Cursor's CLI agent working with your laptop closed: Cursor's Cloud Agents, or the CLI on a box that stays on, signed in there with a link or a QR code, with the permissions to set before you leave it, and what it costs.
- [Run Cursor Cloud Agents on Prized](https://prized.dev/docs/cursor-cloud-agents.md): Run the commands and edits of Cursor Cloud Agents on your box through Cursor's My Machines: start a Cursor worker in your project, then pick the box at cursor.com/agents, in Slack or GitHub, or in Cursor's API.
- [Run Claude Code and Codex on a remote dev box](https://prized.dev/docs/remote-dev-box.md): Run Claude Code or Codex on a cloud Linux box and keep working locally: your project synced both ways, dev servers on localhost, or the box in Cursor or VS Code.
- [Use Cursor or VS Code on a cloud dev box with Remote-SSH](https://prized.dev/docs/cursor-remote-ssh.md): Open a cloud Linux box in Cursor or VS Code over Remote-SSH, with no keys or hosts to set up, and keep Claude Code or Codex running after the editor closes.
- [Use your devcontainer.json](https://prized.dev/docs/devcontainers.md): Run a repo's devcontainer.json on a Prized box with the Dev Containers CLI or VS Code's Reopen in Container: image, features, Docker Compose, Docker in Docker, and create commands, with nothing Prized-specific in the file.
- [Run multiple Claude Code and Codex agents in parallel](https://prized.dev/docs/parallel-agents.md): Run several coding agents at once without slowing your laptop: a git worktree and a session per agent, on one bigger box or a box each, with what each costs.
- [Give your AI agent a persistent cloud computer](https://prized.dev/docs/persistent-cloud-computer.md): A Linux machine your agent keeps coming back to, with its files, installed packages and running processes: create a box through the API, run commands and coding agents on it, let it sleep between sessions, and what it costs.
- [Run Claude Code on a schedule in the cloud](https://prized.dev/docs/run-claude-code-on-a-schedule.md): Run Claude Code every night or every hour on a cloud machine that is on when the schedule fires: cron on a box that stays awake, or a scheduler that wakes a sleeping box through the API, with what each costs.
- [Run Superset's agents on a Prized box](https://prized.dev/docs/superset.md): Add a Prized box as a Superset host, so Superset's parallel agents run on a cloud Linux machine that keeps working while your laptop sleeps, with a size for each number of agents.
- [Run Orca's agents on a Prized box](https://prized.dev/docs/orca.md): Connect Orca to a Prized box over SSH, so its worktrees and coding agents run on a cloud Linux machine: what Orca installs there, the agent sign-ins it needs, dev server ports, and what happens when your laptop sleeps.
- [Use opencode Desktop with a Prized box](https://prized.dev/docs/opencode-desktop.md): Add a Prized box as an SSH server in opencode Desktop, so its sessions run on a cloud Linux machine that keeps working while your laptop sleeps: what Desktop installs there, provider sign-ins on the box, and reconnecting.
- [Run T3 Code's agents on a Prized box](https://prized.dev/docs/t3-code.md): Add a Prized box as an SSH environment in T3 Code, so its Claude Code and Codex threads run on a cloud Linux machine: what T3 Code installs there, the agent CLIs it expects, its browser on Ubuntu 24.04, and worktrees.
- [Run Happy Agent on a Prized box](https://prized.dev/docs/happy.md): Put Happy Agent on a Prized box as a remote server, so the Claude Code and Codex sessions you drive from Happy Desktop or your phone keep running while your laptop sleeps: what it installs, the sign-ins it needs, and keeping the box awake.
- [Run Paseo's agents on a Prized box](https://prized.dev/docs/paseo.md): Run the Paseo daemon on a Prized box and add it as a Remote SSH host, so Paseo's parallel agents work in worktrees on a cloud Linux machine: installing the daemon, connecting, worktrees and ports, and when it is refused.
- [Run Ouroboros on a Prized box](https://prized.dev/docs/ouroboros.md): Install Ouroboros on a Prized box, so its interview, Seed and build loop drives Claude Code on a cloud Linux machine and keeps going with your laptop closed.
- [Run Hermes Agent on a Prized box](https://prized.dev/docs/hermes-agent.md): Keep Hermes Agent and its messaging gateway running around the clock on a Prized box, so it answers on Telegram, Discord, Slack or WhatsApp with your laptop closed.
- [Run OpenClaw on a Prized box](https://prized.dev/docs/openclaw.md): Keep the OpenClaw Gateway running around the clock on a Prized box, so your assistant answers on WhatsApp, Telegram, Discord or Slack with your laptop closed, and open its Control UI on your localhost.
- [Run code from an AI agent in an isolated VM](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-run-agent-code.md): Run an AI agent's commands in a microVM of its own: create a sandbox, run each command with a timeout, move files in and out, and delete it, with what it costs.
- [Run an orchestrator agent with a sandbox per task](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-orchestrator.md): Keep the orchestrator agent on a box and give each builder or task its own sandbox, deleted when the task ends: a Python fan-out script, cleanup, costs, and limits.
- [Fork a running VM to run work in parallel](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-fork-parallel.md): Set up one sandbox, fork it into live copies with the setup done, run a different job in each, collect the results, and delete them all, with what it costs.
- [Run Claude Code with --dangerously-skip-permissions in an isolated VM](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-claude-code-skip-permissions.md): Let Claude Code or Codex run every command without asking, in a cloud microVM that holds only the repo and key you give it: create the sandbox, copy the project in, run the agent, take the diff out, and delete it, with what it costs.
- [Run a sandbox for days without losing your agent's state](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-long-running.md): A sandbox your agent keeps across sessions: run it up to 7 days at a time and extend it, pause it with its memory and processes while the agent is idle, resume it with the next command, and what it costs.
- [Run hosted Firecracker microVMs for AI agents through an API](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-firecracker-api.md): Firecracker microVMs your agent creates, runs commands in, pauses with their memory, forks and deletes through an HTTP API or the CLI, with no hosts, kernels or images of your own to run, billed per second.
- [Use a sandbox as your own template](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-own-template.md): Set up one sandbox with your packages, repo and system libraries, pause it, and start every job as a fork of it, with the install done.
- [Moving from Ona (Gitpod)](https://prized.dev/docs/moving-from-ona.md): Use a Prized box as an Ona or Gitpod alternative before Ona Cloud closes on October 31, 2026: save your work, turn .gitpod.yml, devcontainer.json, and .ona/config.yaml into a setup script, and run it on a box.
- [Moving from Runloop](https://prized.dev/docs/moving-from-runloop.md): Use Prized sandboxes as a Runloop alternative: the Prized command for each Runloop devbox call, a short ported script, and what is not on Prized yet.
- [Moving from E2B](https://prized.dev/docs/moving-from-e2b.md): Run most E2B Python and TypeScript code on Prized sandboxes by changing its imports, map each E2B call to the Prized API and CLI, and see what works differently and what is not on Prized yet.

## Connect

- [Sync](https://prized.dev/docs/sync.md): Mirror a project folder between the box and your machine, both directions and .git included, with edits landing on the other side in under a second.
- [Mount](https://prized.dev/docs/mount.md): Browse the whole box in Finder as a live network drive; nothing is copied and it uses no disk space on your Mac.
- [Ports](https://prized.dev/docs/ports.md): A server on the box opens on your machine as localhost within about two seconds, with no flags or config, and any port can be shared at a link with your workspace's members.
- [Sessions](https://prized.dev/docs/sessions.md): Your shell runs in a persistent session on the box, so a network drop or a closed lid ends only the connection; reconnect and you are where you left off, running processes included.
- [herdr](https://prized.dev/docs/herdr.md): Land in herdr instead of tmux, a terminal multiplexer that shows which of your coding agents is working, waiting on you, or done.
- [Run commands and files](https://prized.dev/docs/exec.md): Run a command on your box and get its exit code, leave one running and follow it live, read any command's output later even with the box paused (not open to every workspace yet), or copy files either way, from the CLI or any script holding an API key, with nothing to install on the box.
- [Desktop](https://prized.dev/docs/desktop.md): A full Linux desktop, or a single Chrome window, from any box in your browser, streamed over VNC inside the private tunnel with nothing opened to the internet.

## Agents

- [Agents](https://prized.dev/docs/agents.md): Start a coding agent on your box, close the laptop, and let it work; it is an ordinary process on a machine you already pay for, so nothing it does is metered.
- [Prompt an agent remotely](https://prized.dev/docs/remote-agents.md): Prompt Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode or pi on your box from your machine or any script, on the model and backend you pick, follow the run as it happens, and interrupt it; the run lives on the box, so nothing depends on your laptop staying open.
- [Codex remote](https://prized.dev/docs/codex.md): Run Codex on your box from the ChatGPT desktop app, with every edit syncing back to your Mac.
- [Claude Desktop remote](https://prized.dev/docs/claude-desktop.md): Run Claude Code on your box from the Claude desktop app's Code tab over SSH, with nothing to install on the box first, and what to know about sign-in, ports and long tasks.

## Boxes

- [Boxes](https://prized.dev/docs/boxes.md): Create, pause, resize, move, and delete boxes: dedicated Linux machines from Nano to Extra Large.
- [Machines](https://prized.dev/docs/machines.md): What a box is made of: its size and disk, Ubuntu 24.04 on a dedicated x86_64 virtual machine, preinstalled software, and the Prized processes beside your work.
- [Snapshots](https://prized.dev/docs/snapshots.md): Take point-in-time images of a box's disk by hand or on a schedule, restore them into new boxes, and fork a running box.
- [Environments](https://prized.dev/docs/environments.md): What a new box inherits, kept as versions: repos to clone, a setup script, env vars, secret files, and which of your credentials it may receive (CLI and API only).
- [Credentials](https://prized.dev/docs/credentials.md): How your agents' and tools' sign-ins reach a box: handed over from your machine through the SSH tunnel, never seen, stored, or proxied by the control plane.
- [Connectors](https://prized.dev/docs/connectors.md): Mount brokered credentials for ClickHouse, OpenAI, Anthropic, GitHub, or Slack into a dev box without writing the real value to its disk.
- [AWS access](https://prized.dev/docs/aws.md): Connect an IAM role once, and every box in your workspace gets an AWS profile that assumes it with a short-lived identity token instead of access keys. In early access.
- [Prized vs GitHub Codespaces, Coder and DevPod](https://prized.dev/docs/boxes-vs-codespaces-coder-devpod.md): How a Prized box compares with GitHub Codespaces, Coder and DevPod as a cloud dev environment: who runs it, devcontainer.json, how long it runs, files and ports, price and free use, each fact from the vendor's own pages, checked October 27, 2026.
- [GitHub Codespaces alternatives](https://prized.dev/docs/codespaces-alternatives.md): Five GitHub Codespaces alternatives for a cloud dev environment (Prized, Coder, DevPod, Google Cloud Workstations, and a VM of your own) with when each fits better and where Codespaces still wins, each fact from the vendor's own pages, checked October 27, 2026.
- [Gitpod and Ona Cloud alternatives](https://prized.dev/docs/gitpod-ona-alternatives.md): Where to take your environments when Ona Cloud, formerly Gitpod, closes on October 31, 2026: Prized, Codex Cloud, GitHub Codespaces, Coder and DevPod, with when each fits better, each fact from the vendor's own pages, checked October 27, 2026.

## Sandboxes

- [Sandboxes](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes.md): Sandboxes (beta): Linux microVMs your code creates through the API or the CLI. Each boots from a template, runs commands and moves files, pauses for the price of its storage, and forks into many live copies.
- [Sandbox API](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-api.md): Every sandbox route: create, list, pause, resume, fork, resize, and delete on the control plane; run commands and move files on the edge; with the fields, states, and error codes.
- [Use Sandboxes from Python or TypeScript](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-python-typescript.md): A small helper for each language that creates a sandbox, runs commands, moves files, forks it, and deletes it. Each is one file with no dependencies.
- [Use Prized from your coding agent](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-coding-agents.md): Teach Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, or another coding agent to run code in Prized sandboxes: install the Prized skill, or paste a short section into AGENTS.md.
- [Run Vercel AI SDK tool calls in a sandbox](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-vercel-ai-sdk.md): Give a Vercel AI SDK agent a runCommand tool: each session gets its own sandbox, the model's commands run there through the Sandbox API, and the sandbox is deleted at the end.
- [Sandboxes FAQ](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-faq.md): Short answers about Sandboxes (beta): starting, forking, isolation, pricing, limits, what is not there yet, and where to get help.
- [Prized vs E2B, Daytona and Modal](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-vs-e2b-daytona-modal.md): How Prized sandboxes compare with E2B, Daytona and Modal on isolation, run length, pause and fork, price, free credit and GPUs, each fact from the vendor's own pages, checked October 22, 2026.
- [E2B alternatives](https://prized.dev/docs/e2b-alternatives.md): Six E2B alternatives for running agent and untrusted code in a sandbox (Prized, Daytona, Modal, Vercel Sandbox, Cloudflare Sandboxes and Sprites) with when each fits better and where E2B still wins, each fact from the vendor's own pages, checked October 27, 2026.
- [Runloop alternatives](https://prized.dev/docs/runloop-alternatives.md): Runloop's transition support ends November 13, 2026. Six Runloop alternatives for agent devboxes (Prized, Tensorlake, Modal, Freestyle, Daytona and E2B) with when each fits better, each fact from the vendor's own pages, checked October 28, 2026.

## Account

- [Dashboard](https://prized.dev/docs/dashboard.md): A map of the signed-in app at prized.dev/dashboard: every screen, its controls, and where each setting lives.
- [Workspaces](https://prized.dev/docs/workspaces.md): One account for the whole team; boxes, balance, and secrets shared by everyone in it.
- [Teams](https://prized.dev/docs/teams.md): Bring your team in by email or with one link, and what a shared workspace adds to one account: roles, policies that bind members, quiet hours, an audit log with a scheduled export, scoped secrets, and a live activity page.
- [Billing](https://prized.dev/docs/billing.md): How a monthly plan ($10 to $1,000, or sized to your boxes past that) becomes credit, what boxes cost by the hour, and what happens when the credit runs out.
- [Limits](https://prized.dev/docs/limits.md): Every hard cap a box or a workspace can hit, and what the refusal looks like.
- [Data retention](https://prized.dev/docs/data-retention.md): What pausing, deleting, and closing keep and remove, how zero data retention changes that, and what Prized retains afterwards.

## Reference

- [CLI reference](https://prized.dev/docs/cli.md): Every prized command, flag, JSON shape, and exit code, on macOS, Linux, and Windows.
- [Sign up from your terminal](https://prized.dev/docs/terminal-signup.md): One command signs you up, starts a box on the free credit, and sets up this machine: npx prized.
- [API reference](https://prized.dev/docs/api.md): Every HTTP route an API key can call: the control plane for boxes and workspace settings, the edge for commands, files, agent prompts, and stopping the desktop.
- [Troubleshooting](https://prized.dev/docs/troubleshooting.md): Symptoms and their fixes, starting with the one command that checks and repairs config, auth, tunnel, SSH, sync, the daemon, and the box agent.
- [Changelog](https://prized.dev/docs/changelog.md): What changed in each release of the prized CLI and box agent, and in the dashboard and the API, newest first.

## Trust

- [Security and trust](https://prized.dev/docs/security.md): How a box is reached, what the control plane can and cannot see, where credentials and secrets go, and how to report a problem.
- [Privacy](https://prized.dev/docs/privacy.md): What Prized records about how you use the product, and what it never records.

## Optional

